Let's see. Yeah, right there.

250
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:08,400
Alright, so, then they play a coordination game.

251
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:11,400
And let's just stick with the same payoffs, because that makes life easy.

252
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:12,400
Alright.

253
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:18,400
Now, notice, so we play, as I said, so we play a prisoner's limit, then we play a coordination game.

254
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:22,400
Is this finite or infinite?

255
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:24,400
Finite.

256
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:29,400
Finite. It is a game which is composed of two games, and two is less than infinity.

257
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:34,400
Alright, so it's just two games, and we snap them together into one big game.

258
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:35,400
Okay.

259
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:40,400
Now, are the original equilibria still possible?

260
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:42,400
And the answer is yes, always.

261
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:47,400
So, whenever you do repetition, it always remains theoretically possible that what happens is,

262
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:50,400
whatever was the equilibrium of the games taken separately, this happens again.

263
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:56,400
So, we have one equilibrium where both play don't cooperate, and then both play left.

264
00:12:56,400 --> 00:12:59,400
Or they both play don't cooperate, and they both play right.

265
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:04,400
So, those are always possibilities where we just take the equilibria in one game,

266
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,400
and the equilibrium in the other game, and we just say those things happen again.

267
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:09,400
And that's always possible.

268
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:18,400
But, there is a Pareto superior outcome that is now possible just because we've snapped together two games.

269
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:21,400
Alright, and this is going to seem really weird to you.

270
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:24,400
Based upon past experience, people find this very strange.

271
00:13:24,400 --> 00:13:30,400
But, I swear to you, this is totally valid in terms of the logic of game theory.

272
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,400
Alright, and here is what I propose.

273
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:40,400
Suppose that each player plays left in the second game, if either player failed to play cooperate in the first game.

274
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:48,400
Right, and otherwise, they both play, and otherwise they both play right.

275
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:51,400
So, in other words, here's what I'm proposing.

276
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:53,400
So, imagine you're playing this game with me.

277
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:55,400
And I say, alright, this is what should happen.

278
00:13:55,400 --> 00:14:00,400
Alright, so, if either of us ever plays don't cooperate, then we both play left.

279
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:05,400
But, if as long as we both, or as soon as, wait, did I say don't, or did I say,

280
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:08,400
if either of us ever fails to cooperate, then we both play left.

281
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:14,400
But, as long as both of us cooperate in turn one, then we both play right in turn two.

282
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:17,400
Alright, now, is this a Nash equilibrium?

283
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:19,400
Right, actually it is.

284
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:20,400
Because, think about this.

285
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,400
Now, you may be saying, oh, well this guy is a dummy.

286
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:24,400
You know what I'm going to do?

287
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:25,400
I'm going to say, yeah, wonderful idea.

288
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:29,400
And then I'm going to go and play don't cooperate in turn one.

289
00:14:29,400 --> 00:14:33,400
Alright, now, as long as you stick to my plan, what do you get?

290
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:37,400
So, you get five in turn one, and you get five in turn two for a total of ten.

291
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:41,400
We're not doing any discounting yet, although we will do discounting as we proceed.

292
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:43,400
Anyway, so you just figure there's no discounting.

293
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,400
So, then we both get ten.

294
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:46,400
Sounds good.

295
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:51,400
Now, you may be thinking, ha, sucker, I'm going to go and play don't cooperate in turn one.

296
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:53,400
And then what are you going to do about it?

297
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:55,400
Well, let's find out what happens.

298
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:57,400
So, I play don't cooperate in turn one, then I get six.

299
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:03,400
And now, based upon what we said, we're both going to play left.

300
00:15:03,400 --> 00:15:06,400
Now, you may say, well, I'm not going to go along with that and play left.

301
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:08,400
I'm going to play right.

302
00:15:08,400 --> 00:15:10,400
So, but here's the problem.

303
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:15,400
Given that I'm playing left in turn two, what do you get when you play right in turn two?

304
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:16,400
Zero.

305
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:20,400
So, if you go and deviate, instead of getting ten, you get six.

306
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:24,400
And you say, fine, fine, I'll go and stick with left in turn one and turn two.

307
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:30,400
Then, instead of getting ten from doing it my way, you get six plus three, you get nine doing it your way.

308
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:33,400
And you have actually hurt yourself.

309
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:36,400
Right now, you may think there's something weird here.

310
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:40,400
And you know, so the only weird thing here is Nash equilibrium itself.

311
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,400
Well, so that may be weird.

312
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:48,400
The idea here is, remember, in Nash equilibrium, all you can do, or when you're testing Nash equilibrium,

313
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:53,400
the only question you get to ask is, holding constant with what the other person does,

314
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:56,400
can I do better if I do something different?

315
00:15:56,400 --> 00:16:05,400
So, given that you know that I'm going to play left, if anyone failed to play cooperate in turn one,

316
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:09,400
then your best thing, given what I'm doing in turn two, is to play left.

317
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:14,400
And that ruins all the benefit of stabbing me in the back in turn one.

318
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:17,400
Right, and that's the amazing thing.

319
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:20,400
And notice, this is only a two-term game.

320
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:23,400
This is not a fancy infinite game or anything like that.

321
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:27,400
All I've done is I've taken two games, snapped them together,

322
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:34,400
and I have shown that there is a new equilibrium which is greater or superior to what we're able to do if the games are separate.

323
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:39,400
Remember, if the games are separate, then the only Nash equilibrium is to play don't in that game.

324
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:41,400
And then we have two over here.

325
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:48,400
But now that I have suggested conditioning behavior in turn two, what happened in turn one,

326
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:52,400
we've actually expanded the set of sustainable equilibrium.

327
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:55,400
And now it's possible to have us cooperate in turn one,

328
00:16:55,400 --> 00:17:02,400
and then this weird threat of playing left in turn two leads us to go and both play right.

329
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:08,400
It leads us to not deviate in turn one, and then we both do that in the second turn.

330
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:11,400
Now, here's the thing that's really odd.

331
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:16,400
It's like, well, but why is it that you would go and carry out this punishment in turn two?

332
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:19,400
And the answer is, we're doing it together in turn two.

333
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:22,400
We're punishing ourselves in turn two.

334
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:24,400
There's collective punishment in turn two.

335
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:34,400
And given that we expect collective punishment to happen, what can either one of us do to avert that collective punishment?

336
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:39,400
Well, what can we do in turn two in order to prevent the collective punishment from hitting us both?

337
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:41,400
Both go right.

338
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:46,400
But can one person go to the – is it possible for one person to both go right?

339
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:50,400
No. One person only controls his own actions.

340
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:54,400
So as long as you believe that I'm going to do what I originally said,

341
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:59,400
then you actually don't want to go and deviate from this.

342
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:05,400
So, again, if you think about the way collective punishment works, we all suffer for the sins of the one.

343
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:08,400
One person goes and messes up.

344
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:11,400
One person cheats on the exam, so I fail the whole class.

345
00:18:11,400 --> 00:18:15,400
Right? We're not doing that, but imagine that.

346
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:24,400
So if this is happening, what can one person do in order to avert this disaster?

347
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:27,400
Well, how about don't cheat in the first place?

348
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:31,400
But once it's happening, then there's no way to avert it any longer.

349
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:33,400
One person can't do it.

350
00:18:33,400 --> 00:18:38,400
So each person then has to live with the consequences that have occurred.

351
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:40,400
And that's the idea.

352
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:44,400
Now, of course, you may say, but the person who gets stabbed in the back really suffers.

353
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:47,400
So the person who gets stabbed in the back gets zero in turn one,

354
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:51,400
and gets three in turn two, so they go from getting ten to three.

355
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:57,400
But remember, in Nash equilibrium, are we still going to be worrying about whether the other person will suffer?

356
00:18:57,400 --> 00:18:59,400
No, we're worrying about whether we will suffer.

357
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:03,400
Do I have an incentive to do something different from what was proposed?

358
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:06,400
And remember, am I saying this is the only equilibrium?

359
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:09,400
This weird thing that I just told you, is that the only equilibrium?

360
00:19:09,400 --> 00:19:10,400
Not at all.

361
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:14,400
It's just a new one, it's an extra one that we didn't have before which could happen.

362
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:18,400
It's so weird that that feels like, you think that I came up with this one on my own?

363
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:19,400
No, someone told it to me.

364
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:23,400
When I was in your shoes, the rest of us said, this is something else that could happen.

365
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:25,400
I'm like, what? That's really weird.

366
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:30,400
Anyway, now that we know this, if you ever find yourself in this situation, you know what to do.

367
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:33,400
You go and say, all right, here's what I propose.

368
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,400
We both go our way in turn one.

369
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:38,400
And then if either person fails to do that, then in turn two we both lay left.

370
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:42,400
And then neither of us will want to do anything different from what I just said,

371
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:45,400
given that we expect that both of us will do that.

372
00:19:45,400 --> 00:19:49,400
And if the other person doesn't seem convinced or seems like, yeah, yeah, great idea,

373
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:52,400
if they agree too quickly, say, no, no, no, you think that I'm a sucker, but I'm not a sucker.

374
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:55,400
I've worked this through.

375
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,400
You won't want to do something different.

376
00:19:58,400 --> 00:20:08,400
And if they say, you won't really play left in turn two, you won't want to do it.


